  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Prove that $\\log_2 3$ is irrational.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\log_2 3$ is irrational.\n\nA rational value $\\frac{p}{q}$ would give $3^q=2^p$, contradicting the opposite parities of these positive integer powers.",
      "worked": "Suppose $\\log_2 3=\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p,q$ are positive integers. This is possible under the rationality assumption because $\\log_2 3>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&=2^{p/q}\\\\3^q&=2^p.\\end{align*}$$\nThe left-hand side is odd and the right-hand side is even, a contradiction. Therefore $\\log_2 3$ is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
